
[[installing-modules]]
=== Installing modules

Every installed module belongs to a *library*. The standard library, `std`, is
hosted on GitHub under the https://github.com/morloclib[morloclib]
organization, and the bioinformatics library, `bio`, under
https://github.com/morloc-bio[morloc-bio]. A module you publish yourself
belongs to a library you name, usually your GitHub user name. Modules are
installed with the `morloc install` command:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc install internal
$ morloc install root
$ morloc install root-cpp
$ morloc install root-py
$ morloc install root-r
----

The positional arguments to `morloc install` are *install strings*. Each
describes one module to install and optionally pins it to a specific commit,
branch, or release tag. More than one install string may be passed in a single
invocation, in which case each is installed in turn (any transitive dependencies
are also installed automatically).

[source, text]
----
INSTALL  := TARGET [ "@" [FORM ":"] REF ]
TARGET   := <name>                    # a standard library module
          | <library>/<name>          # a module of a known library
          | REMOTE ":" <owner>/<repo> # a repository on a host
          | <local-path>              # must start with '.', '/', or '~'
REMOTE   := "github" | "gitlab" | "bitbucket" | "codeberg" | "azure"
FORM     := "hash" | "branch" | "version" | "tag"   # 'tag' is an alias of 'version'
REF      := <hexhash>        # seven or more hex digits
          | <semver>         # [v]M.m[.p][-prerelease][+build]
          | <branch>         # a valid git branch name
----

Without an explicit `FORM:` prefix the `REF` is auto-detected by
trying hash first, then version, then branch. If no `@REF` is given
the default branch is used. Clones use HTTPS unless `--ssh` is passed.

The module's `package.yaml` must give its `name` and its `library`, and the
repository must be named after the module. Where the module comes from
depends on the form:

 - A bare name like `root` is the standard library's `root`, from
   `github.com/morloclib/root`.
 - `library/name` fetches from the library's *origin*, the host and owner its
   modules live under: `bio/convert` is `github.com/morloc-bio/convert`.
 - `github:weena/calendar` fetches that repository. If its `package.yaml`
   says `library: weena` and no library `weena` is known yet, Morloc records
   `weena` as coming from `github:weena`, so `weena/<name>` works from then
   on. `gitlab:` is gitlab.com, `codeberg:` is codeberg.org, `bitbucket:` is
   bitbucket.org.
 - A local path must begin with `.`, `/`, or `~`. Everything from the path
   start to the first `@` is the path, and any `@REF` that follows is honored
   only when the local directory is itself a git repository -- otherwise the
   working tree is copied verbatim (filtering `.git` and gitignored files)
   and the ref is silently dropped. A local install records no origin.

The origins of `std` and `bio` are built in and cannot be changed. A library
named something other than its owner is recorded with
`morloc library add <library> <host>:<owner>`, and `morloc library list`
shows every known library. A package fetched from anywhere but its library's
origin is refused.

.Install string examples
[cols="2,3", options="header"]
|===
| Install string | Effect

| `root`
| `root` from `morloclib/root` on GitHub (default branch)

| `root root-py math`
| install three modules in one invocation

| `bio/convert`
| `convert` from the `bio` library's origin, `morloc-bio/convert`

| `github:weena/calendar`
| `weena/calendar` on GitHub (default branch)

| `weena/calendar`
| the same, once the library `weena` is known

| `gitlab:weena/calendar`
| `weena/calendar` on gitlab.com

| `codeberg:weena/foo`
| `weena/foo` on codeberg.org

| `weena/calendar@1.0`
| release tag `1.0` (semver auto-detected)

| `weena/calendar@v1.0.0`
| release tag `v1.0.0`

| `weena/calendar@version:1.0.0`
| explicit release-tag form

| `weena/calendar@tag:1.0.0`
| same as `@version:1.0.0` (`tag` is an alias)

| `weena/calendar@a1b2c3d`
| commit `a1b2c3d` (hash auto-detected at >= 7 hex chars)

| `weena/calendar@hash:abc1234`
| explicit commit-hash form

| `weena/calendar@dev`
| branch `dev` (auto-detected after hash/version fail)

| `weena/calendar@branch:feature/x`
| explicit branch form (allows `/` in the branch name)

| `gitlab:weena/foo@version:1.0`
| remote prefix combined with an explicit release tag

| `.`
| install from the current working directory

| `./mymod`
| install from a relative path

| `~/code/mymod`
| install from a tilde-expanded path

| `/abs/path/to/mod`
| install from an absolute path

| `./mymod@branch:dev`
| install branch `dev` of a local git repo

| `./mymod@v0.2.0`
| install tag `v0.2.0` of a local git repo
|===

The `morloc install -h` help screen carries a shorter example block
for quick reference.

Installed modules are stored in
`~/.local/share/morloc/src/morloc/plane/<library>/<module>/` (under
`$MORLOC_STATE` instead of `~/.local/share/morloc` when that is set) and can be imported in
any Morloc program, as <<importing-modules>> describes.

To view the modules that are currently installed, you can run `morloc
list`. This will list all installed modules, their version, and their short
descriptions. Adding the `-v` option additionally prints the types of all
exported terms.

To view the exports of one module, add a pattern. It matches any module whose
name contains the pattern's characters in order, so `il` would match
`internal` too:

[source, console]
----
$ morloc list -v internal
Modules:
  internal 0.6.0  The universal and required implicit Morloc module
    $ :: (b -> a) -> b -> a
    . :: (b -> a) -> (c -> b) -> c -> a
...
----


[[configuring-cpp-build]]
==== Configuring the {cpp} build

When a Morloc program contains {cpp} sources, the compiler invokes `g++`
(or whatever `$CXX` resolves to) to build the {cpp} side of the program.
Three optional `package.yaml` fields tune that build: two structured
fields (`cpp-version` and `cpp-deps`) that translate into specific
flag patterns, and one verbatim field (`cxx-flags`) that passes
arbitrary flags through unchanged. For build steps that go beyond
flag-tweaking, a fourth field -- `setup` -- runs a shell script at
install time.

`cpp-version: 20`::
Selects the {cpp} standard. Translates directly into a `-std=c++NN`
flag (default `20`).
+
[source,yaml]
----
cpp-version: 20
----

`cpp-deps`::
Names the libraries the {cpp} code uses. The environment provisions each
one, and an entry may list the headers your code includes (`import`) and
the names the pool links (`libs`, each `hts` becoming `-lhts`). Morloc
refuses to build when a listed header cannot be found, and in a managed
environment it puts the environment's headers and libraries on the
compile line itself. <<cpp>> covers the details.
+
[source,yaml]
----
cpp-deps:
  htslib: {version: ">=1.20", import: htslib/sam.h, libs: [hts]}
----

`cxx-flags: [-O3, -DENABLE_FAST_PATH]`::
A free-form list of flags appended verbatim to the compile command.
Use this for anything the structured fields above don't cover --
optimization levels, architecture targeting, preprocessor defines,
warning controls, and so on. Each list element is one argument, so no
quoting or word-splitting is needed. Repeated elements are merged, so
write a flag that takes a value as one element (`-Wl,-rpath,DIR`, not
`-Xlinker` twice). A library the environment does not provide, such as
the system's zlib or one a `setup` script builds, is linked here
(`-lz`).
+
[source,yaml]
----
cxx-flags:
  - -O3
  - -march=native
  - -DENABLE_FAST_PATH
----

The three fields combine:

[source,yaml]
----
name: vector-ops
version: 0.1.0
cpp-version: 20
cpp-deps:
  eigen: {version: "*", import: Eigen/Dense}
cxx-flags:
  - -O3
  - -march=native
  - -DEIGEN_NO_DEBUG
----

When one module depends on another, the downstream module inherits
all three fields from its dependencies: a consumer of `vector-ops`
above gets Eigen provisioned and its header checked, and the listed
`cxx-flags` on its own {cpp} compile line.

For build steps that go beyond what these flags can express -- running
`cmake`, building a vendored library from source, fetching pre-built
artifacts, or installing a Python or R package -- use the more general
`setup` field, which names a shell script that runs once at install
time:

[source,yaml]
----
name: my-package
setup: scripts/install.sh
----

The script runs after the module's source has been laid down on disk
and after morloc-level dependencies have been installed, with these
environment variables set:

`MORLOC_HOME`:: Morloc's install root (default `~/.local/share/morloc`).
`MORLOC_MODULE_NAME`, `MORLOC_MODULE_LIBRARY`, `MORLOC_MODULE_VERSION`::
name, library and version from this `package.yaml`.
`MORLOC_MODULE_DIR`:: absolute path to the installed module's
directory; also the script's working directory.
`MORLOC_PLANE_DIR`:: the directory of installed modules, one subdirectory
per library.

A non-zero exit fails the install. The path must be relative to the
module root and must not contain `..` segments. Prefer the three
structured fields above when they suffice -- a setup script is more
powerful but also more code to maintain.


[[universal-library]]
=== The universal library

A module may export types, typeclasses, and function signatures but no
implementations. Such a module would be completely language agnostic. A good
approach to building libraries in the Morloc ecosystem is to write one module
that defines all types, then $n$ modules for language-specific implementations
that import the type module, and then one module to import and merge all
implementations. This is the approach taken by the `root` module and by other
core libraries.

In the future, when hundreds of languages are supported, and when possibly some
functions may even have many implementations per language, it will be desirable
to have finer control over what functions are used. One solution would be to add
filters to the import statement. Thus the import expressions would be a sort of
query. Alternatively, constraints could be added at the function level, and thus
the entire Morloc script would be a query over the universal library. This would
be especially useful when imported types are expressed as unknowns to be
inferred by usage.


